From: =?utf-8?b?Ik1hcmNvIFRyZXZpc2FuIChUcmV2acOxbyki?= <mail@3v1n0.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:57:28 +0200
Subject: userVerifier: Do not allow a auth service to reset if not in control

Authentication services share the same user verifier. A reset emitted by any
of them is propagated to the auth prompt, where it cancels the current
verification and starts it again. It is therefore a service-scoped control
operation, not only a UI update.

This allowed a background service to interrupt the foreground service. For
example, after selecting one mechanism, a reset emitted by another service
could cancel its conversation and restart verification. Conversely, when
switching mechanisms, the new service may synchronously reset to start its
conversation while the previous service is still considered in control.

Include the emitting service name in reset parameters and apply the same
foreground-service filtering used for other auth-service signals. Set the
new foreground service before invoking its selection handler, because that
handler may synchronously reset to start its conversation. If no service
accepts the requested mechanism, restore the previous foreground service.

Finally, preserve the emitting service across a soft reset. A soft reset is
used to restart verification for the newly selected mechanism, so clearing
its service name would make the next verification fall back to another
auto-selectable service instead of continuing with the selected mechanism.

This prevents background services or started-but-not-selected services from
resetting the active conversation while allowing a foreground service to
restart and take control of its own verification.
